


PGFx25.s - Pretty Good Folding for TSE 2.5

/***
This version of PGF 1.054 was adapted for use on TSE 2.5.
 Ignore all references to:
 1. hiliting - used to hilite fold codes and reminder ribbon
 2. fold code protection - used to prevent accidental editing of fold
    codes
 3. reminder ribbon - used to remind user of assigned menu/termination keys
 4. hooks - used to act on the above features

In this initial adaptation, those would not work with TSE 2.5. Such
fetures may be worked out in the future.
***/

PGF.s - Pretty Good Folding

PGF Version 1.054 was written with and for TSEPRO 4.4.

After compiling and executing, this macro will fold any number of
paragraphs and marked line blocks up to 1.7 million.

The macro will fold text anywhere in a file in any number of files.

As usual, if you decide to play with this macro, do it using throwaway
text and report anything unusual.

Once folded, text can be expanded in any order.

All folded text from all sources are kept in one fold buffer file.

Once text is folded, the top text line remains in the original file and
a unique fold code is inserted at columns 1-3. This code is continuously
hilited and the macro protects it from being changed at the cursor. Care
must be taken while using Replace() and deleting text so that the codes
are not lost.

The macro operations are intuitive and controlled using either the menu
or the assigned keys for each operation.

For the safety of the folded text, the macro automatically saves the
source file and the fold buffer file. This means that you can exit TSE
today and load a file containing folded text tomorrow and still be able
to expand that text using this macro. (The automatic save feature can be
toggled off at the menu for experimentation.)

The menu operations:
=============================

Fold Paragraph or Block To One Fold Line
  This command will fold a paragraph or a marked line block of any size
  to one fold line. The marked block can include many paragraphs or a
  whole file.
  1. To fold a paragraph:
    a. place cursor on any text line in the paragraph
    b. execute this command
    c. Notes:
      1) the top line of the paragraph cannot be a line that has
         already been folded and contains fold codes.
      2) but, other lines in the paragraph could already have been
         folded and will be folded with the paragraph.
      3) single line paragraphs or blocks are not folded
  2. To fold a marked line block:
    a. mark all of the text to be folded
    b. execute this command
    c. Notes:
      1) blank lines at the top and bottom of the marked block will not
         be included in the fold
      2) the top text line in the marked block must not have already
         been folded and this command will be cancelled if so
      3) the marked block may contain other folded text lines and they
         will be included in the folded text
      4) single line paragraphs or block are not folded
Fold Paragraphs To Multiple Fold Lines
  This command will fold all paragraphs in a marked line block to
  individual folds. Thus, if you have 10 paragraphs in the marked block,
  the macro will produce 10 fold lines, one for each paragraph
  1. To fold a marked block:
    a. line block mark the paragraphs to be folded
    b. blank lines at top and bottom will not be folded
    c. the top line of the block must not have already been folded
    d. execute this command
    e. Notes:
      1) single line paragraphs are not folded
      2) this command will ignore paragraphs that have already been
         folded
  2. if no block is marked, this command will fold all paragraphs in
     the current file

Expand Folded Text At Cursor Line
  1. This command will expand the folded paragraph or block at the cursor
     line.
  2. It will only expand the fold that actually happened when the
     paragraph or marked block was folded.
  3. Other folds that were present when the fold took place will still
     remain folded.

Expand All Folded Text
  1. This command will expand all folds in a marked block
  2. The marked block could either be one fold line or multiple fold
     lines.
  3. ALL folds in the marked block will be expanded.
  4. If a block is not marked, all folds in the current file will be
     expanded.

Find Next Folded Text
  This command will look for the next fold code in the current file.
  Once the command is given, you can use RepeatFind backward or forward
  to find other fold codes. (The Repeat finds will only work until your
  next different Find() command.)

Show All Fold Locations
  This command will search all loaded files for fold codes and display
  them using the 'v' FindView option. It does not show the fold codes in
  the fold buffer.

View The PGF Fold Buffer
  This command opens the PGF Fold Buffer. While there, you can view the
  existing folded text segments and edit any of the text. (NOTE: DO NOT
  edit or change the lines containing the fold codes or the fold
  separation lines.)

Toggle Reminder Ribbon On/Off
  The Reminder Ribbon shows the current version of this macro, the
  assigned key for the Menu, the assigned key to terminate the macro,
  and the current number of folds.

Toggle AutoSave On/Off
  The AutoSave feature is intended to prevent the loss of text that has
  been folded in case of accidental shut down, etc.

  This command will toggle the AutoSave feature on and off. The default
  state is ON, which means that the macro saves the current file and the
  fold buffer file to disk every time you fold or expand text.

Terminate Macro
  This command terminates the macro and turns off all of its features,
  including the highlighting and protection of the fold codes.

  Since the folded text files and the fold buffer have been
  automatically saved, you can exit TSE and return another time and
  execute the macro and recover the folded text.

The Assigned Keys:
========================

<ctrlshift p><p>                            // execute/terminate the macro
<ctrlshift p><m> mpgfmenu()                 // menu
<ctrlshift p><f> mpgffold2one()             // fold to one
<ctrlshift p><e> mpgfexpand()               // expand marked
<ctrlshift p><-> mpgffold2many()            // fold to many
<ctrlshift p><=> mpgfexpandall()            // expand all
<ctrlshift p><g> mpgffindcode()             // find next fold code
<ctrlshift p><s> mpgfshowallfoldlocations() // show fold code locations
<ctrlshift p><v> mpgfview()                 // view the fold buffer

The assigned keys are also shown in the menu.

Enjoy.

